# Copy to .env for local development. `make dev` / docker-compose read these.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://app:app@localhost:5432/app
# `make test` points #[sqlx::test] here instead: same server, but its bookkeeping
# schema stays out of the dev `app` database. (A bare `cargo test` reads
# DATABASE_URL above via .env — works, but writes that schema into `app`.)
TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgres://app:app@localhost:5432/postgres
APP_HOST=0.0.0.0
APP_PORT=8080
APP_ENV=dev
# Max size of the Postgres connection pool.
APP_DB_MAX_CONNECTIONS=10
# Allowed browser origin for the Vite dev server (CORS, dev only).
APP_CORS_ORIGIN=http://localhost:5173
# Dev-only JWT signing/verification secret (HS256). Used only when APP_JWT_ISSUER
# is unset. Rotate for real deployments.
APP_JWT_SECRET=dev-only-insecure-secret-change-me
# Subjects (the token's `sub`), comma-separated, that always hold `admin` whatever
# `access` assigns, and can't be unassigned it: how a fresh deployment gets its
# first admin, who then assigns roles to others. While it is empty, the last
# assigned `admin` can't be unassigned. In dev, the seeds assign the dev sign-in
# (frontend/vite.config.ts) `admin`, or, with no seed that does, this names it.
APP_BOOTSTRAP_ADMINS=
# Production JWT: set APP_JWT_ISSUER to an OIDC issuer to verify tokens against its
# published keys (JWKS), discovered at {issuer}/.well-known/openid-configuration.
# This replaces the HS256 secret path (verify-only; no token issuance). Optionally
# set APP_JWT_AUDIENCE to require a matching `aud` claim.
# APP_JWT_ISSUER=https://your-tenant.example.auth0.com/
# APP_JWT_AUDIENCE=https://api.example.com
# How the embedded SPA signs people in at APP_JWT_ISSUER, served to it at
# GET /auth/config (404 while APP_JWT_ISSUER is unset, when the SPA skips sign-in,
# as in `make dev`). Register the SPA at the provider as a public client: a
# single-page application using the authorization code flow with PKCE and no
# client secret, with redirect URI <origin>/auth/callback (and
# <origin>/auth/signed-out as its sign-out redirect), e.g.
# https://app.example.com/auth/callback. The SPA renews tokens with that same
# redirect URI: with a refresh token when the provider issues one (allow refresh
# tokens for the client; some providers need offline_access, below), otherwise
# silently in a hidden iframe at <origin>/auth/callback with prompt=none, which
# needs the provider's session cookie (browsers that block third-party cookies
# refuse it, and the user then signs in again, coming back to the same page).
# APP_OIDC_CLIENT_ID is that client's id. In prod, a server built with the SPA
# embedded refuses to start with APP_JWT_ISSUER set and this unset.
# APP_OIDC_CLIENT_ID=your-spa-client-id
# Scopes to request beyond `openid profile email`, space-separated (quote them),
# e.g. offline_access for a refresh token, or Entra's api://<app-id>/.default.
# APP_OIDC_SCOPES="offline_access"
# Extra authorization request parameters, space-separated key=value pairs (quote
# them), e.g. Auth0's audience, without which it issues no JWT access token.
# The server refuses to start on one that isn't key=value.
# APP_OIDC_EXTRA_PARAMS="audience=https://api.example.com"
# The app's own client ids at the issuer, comma- or space-separated. A token
# another client obtained (its client_id, else azp) names that client as the
# agent acting for its subject; a client-credentials agent is its own client. A
# token carrying an RFC 8693 `act` claim names its agent whatever the client.
# Unset, APP_OIDC_CLIENT_ID alone.
# APP_FIRST_PARTY_CLIENTS=your-spa-client-id,your-mobile-client-id
# Where clients reach the server, which the sign-in metadata it serves MCP clients
# names (with APP_JWT_ISSUER set). Unset, each request's Host and X-Forwarded-*
# headers say.
# APP_PUBLIC_URL=https://app.example.com
# The scope a token must grant to use /mcp (e.g. a Cognito resource server's
# custom scope), listed in its protected resource metadata. A token without it
# is refused 403 insufficient_scope, unless its aud is the MCP resource's URL
# (RFC 8707). Unset, any valid token is taken.
# APP_MCP_SCOPE=https://api.example.com/mcp
# Invitations (Spec 0012, ADR 0048): the Cognito user pool `access` creates
# invitees in, `<region>_<id>`, which names its region. Unset, inviting is off and
# its operations answer 501. The server reads AWS credentials from the default
# chain (environment, profile, web identity, ECS or IMDS), and needs
# cognito-idp:AdminCreateUser, AdminGetUser, AdminDeleteUser and DescribeUserPool
# on the pool. Set the pool's AllowAdminCreateUserOnly, so an invitation is the
# only way in. Cognito's default email sender is limited to about 50 a day:
# configure SES on the pool before inviting many.
# COGNITO_USER_POOL_ID=ap-southeast-2_AbCdEfGhI
# A stand-in for Cognito's API instead of AWS's, as `make e2e`'s fake-oidc serves.
# COGNITO_ENDPOINT=http://127.0.0.1:9000/cognito
# How long the `worker` bin waits between polls of the outbox and job queue.
APP_WORKER_POLL_MS=1000
# How often, in seconds, each `worker` compacts the outbox (ADR 0033): deletes
# relayed events older than their topic's retention, keeping each key's latest.
# Workers don't coordinate, so N replicas compact up to N times as often.
APP_OUTBOX_COMPACT_EVERY=3600
# The Redis or Valkey that server instances and workers broadcast to each other
# over (ADR 0039): docker compose's `redis` here (`make dev` starts it); in
# production ElastiCache Serverless for Valkey, over TLS and with credentials:
# rediss://<user>:<password>@<endpoint>:6379. Give the server and the worker the
# same one. Required once any module broadcasts: they refuse to start without it.
APP_REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379
# The CA certificate (PEM) to check a rediss:// Redis's certificate against, in
# place of the public roots, for one signed by a private CA. ElastiCache's need
# none.
# APP_REDIS_CA="-----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----..."
RUST_LOG=info,sqlx=warn
# Traces and metrics over OTLP (OpenTelemetry), configured only by the standard
# variables. Unset (the default), nothing is exported and log lines carry no trace_id.
# Set, every log line inside a span carries its trace_id and span_id. RUST_LOG
# also limits which spans are exported: at `warn`, no request spans are.
# Locally, `make dev-observability` starts compose's `observability` profile, a
# Grafana with Tempo and Prometheus (grafana/otel-lgtm) whose UI is at
# http://localhost:3000, and runs `make dev` exporting to it at the endpoint
# below. Or start it with `docker compose --profile observability up -d` and
# uncomment the endpoint for plain `make dev`.
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:4318
# http/protobuf (the default) or http/json; grpc isn't built in.
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_PROTOCOL=http/protobuf
# Headers on every export, key=value pairs comma-separated, e.g. a vendor's API key.
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS=authorization=Basic%20...
# Defaults to the binary: server, worker or migrate. The app's name and version
# are always resource attributes (service.namespace, service.version).
# OTEL_SERVICE_NAME=server
# More resource attributes, key=value pairs comma-separated.
# OTEL_RESOURCE_ATTRIBUTES=deployment.environment.name=staging
# Parent-based, always on by default: a request continues its caller's sampling
# decision (`traceparent`). Lower it with, e.g., parentbased_traceidratio and 0.1.
# OTEL_TRACES_SAMPLER=parentbased_always_on
# OTEL_TRACES_SAMPLER_ARG=
# How often metrics are exported, in milliseconds.
# OTEL_METRIC_EXPORT_INTERVAL=60000
# The assistant (Spec 0012): off without a model id. With an Anthropic credential it calls
# Anthropic's API: ANTHROPIC_API_KEY (x-api-key) or ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN (an OAuth or bearer
# token), the key first; ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL for a gateway.
# APP_AGENT_MODEL=claude-opus-5-5
# ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN=
# Without one it calls Bedrock, region and credentials from the AWS SDK's default chain
# (AWS_REGION, AWS_PROFILE, ...), with a Bedrock model or inference-profile id.
# APP_AGENT_MODEL=eu.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-5-20250929-v1:0
# APP_AGENT_MAX_TOOL_CALLS=10
# APP_AGENT_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS=4096
# APP_AGENT_HISTORY_MESSAGES=50
# sqlx compiles against the checked-in .sqlx cache by default (no live DB needed).
SQLX_OFFLINE=true
